## Project

`sq_` is a browser-only 3D squash game: WSF-dimension court, Rapier physics, Tron neon aesthetic, two-button input (Space = charge-and-release shot, Shift = chase), PARS-11 best-of-3 scoring.

**Stack:** Vite 6 · React 19 · React Three Fiber 9 + Three.js r182 + drei + postprocessing · Rapier at a fixed 1/60 timestep · Zustand 5 · TypeScript 5.7 strict. Vitest for tests.

**Scope:** entirely client-side. No backend, no database, no auth, no persistence, no network calls.

**Constraints:**
- `systems/` stays pure and framework-free; `hooks/` bridges to R3F; `components/` is declarative JSX plus refs for imperative physics.
- Per-frame work goes in `useFrame`, never `useEffect`. Physics state lives in Rapier bodies, never React state.
- All shared game state lives in the single Zustand store (`src/stores/gameStore.ts`) as string-union state machines.
- Rules logic follows official WSF/PARS references — verify against them before changing scoring or serve behaviour.
- Style: no semicolons, single quotes, 2-space indent, explicit return types on exports. No CSS framework; UI overlays use inline styles.
- Prefer relative imports for consistency with the existing code. The `@/*` alias works (`vite.config.ts`); do not "fix" an `@/` import you find.
- Verify with `npm run typecheck`, `npm run lint` and `npm test` (Vitest, no config file). Rules and geometry — scoring, interference, AI movement bounds, shot ballistics — belong in tests; reserve manual play-testing for what only eyes can judge (bloom, trail, HUD legibility, model scale).
- `systems/court.ts` holds the court dimensions. Do not import them from `components/Court.tsx`; that made pure modules pull in React, drei and Rapier, and left them untestable outside a browser.
- Button A (Space / right touch zone) means different things per phase. Its logic lives in `usePhaseInput` and is registered via `registerButtonAActions` in `useInput.ts`; keyboard and touch both call `pressButtonAction` / `releaseButtonAction`. Never bind game logic directly to a key event — it will silently skip touch.
- While button A is held, aim is a separate axis (arrows / A·D, or horizontal drag on the right touch zone) routed through the input store; charge duration is power only.
- The ball can be struck by the racquet sensor and by the per-frame proximity check. `useHitHandlers` enforces a shared cooldown and the `canHit` rule at that single choke point; keep new hit routes going through it.
- Colors live in `src/theme/colors.ts` + `src/theme/tokens.css` (void / player cyan / opponent orange / hotter ball orange). Three.js reads `HEX.*`; HUD reads CSS vars. Bloom shipped in Phase 2 — authored opacities go through `displayAlpha` in `src/config.ts`. Bounce feel is `BOUNCE_PROFILES` / `?bounce=`. Largest remaining items: Rapier is ~75% of the bundle, and `Scene` still re-renders often.

